{"builder":"Goal: make packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js accept several files and stdin, for issue https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104. Open one pull request.\n\nContext: packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js takes one file at the moment (process.argv[2]). It reads the file, calls lint(text) from packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js and prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` for each bad row, replacing newlines in the error with spaces. Exit codes are 0 for clean, 1 for bad rows and 2 for usage or read errors. The tests are in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js and packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js and use node:test. They spawn the bin with spawnSync.\n\nSteps:\n1. Do not touch packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js. lint(text) must stay byte-for-byte unchanged.\n2. Rewrite packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js so it does the following (plain ES modules, no dependencies):\n   - Take every argument in process.argv.slice(2).\n   - No arguments: write a usage line to stderr, for example `usage: jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]` (mention that `-` means stdin), and exit 2.\n   - More than one `-` argument: write a usage error to stderr and exit 2. Do this check before reading anything.\n   - Otherwise go through the arguments in the order given. For `-`, read stdin with readFileSync(0, 'utf8') and label it `-`. For any other argument, call readFileSync(arg, 'utf8') and use the argument exactly as given for the label.\n   - If a read fails, write `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` to stderr, note the failure and go on to the next file. Do not exit early.\n   - For each file that reads, print `<label>:<line>: <error>` lines to stdout in file order, then in row order, in the same format as now (newlines in the error become spaces).\n   - Exit status: 2 if any file could not be read; otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad; otherwise 0.\n   - Keep the behaviour for one file the same as before, so the existing tests still pass.\n3. Add node:test tests in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js (you may also use a new test file under packages/jsonl-lint/test/). Use the existing spawnSync helper and pass `input` for stdin. Cover each case:\n   - two clean files: exit 0, no stdout\n   - one bad file among several clean ones: exit 1, only that file's lines with its name as the prefix\n   - stdin via `-` with clean input (exit 0) and with bad input (exit 1, lines prefixed `-:`)\n   - a missing file among good ones: exit 2, stderr names the missing file, and the other files are still checked and reported (include a bad file so its lines still appear on stdout)\n   - two `-` arguments: exit 2 with an error on stderr\n   - no arguments: exit 2 with usage on stderr\n   Keep the existing tests.\n4. Update packages/jsonl-lint/README.md:\n   - Usage becomes `node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...]`.\n   - Say that `-` reads stdin, is reported as `-`, and may appear only once.\n   - Say that files are checked in order.\n   - State the exit codes: 0 when every file is clean; 1 when any row in any file is bad; 2 when no file is named, `-` is given twice, or any file can't be read (error on stderr, the other files are still checked).\n5. Run `npm test` at the repository root and make sure it passes.\n6. Commit, push and open one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. The PR body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Wait for the `test` workflow (.github/workflows/test.yml) to run on the PR head, and fix anything that fails until it is green.","verifier":"Check out the pull request's head and run every check from the repository root. Make a scratch directory, for example `T=$(mktemp -d)`, holding:\n- `$T/a.jsonl` containing `{\"a\":1}\\n`\n- `$T/b.jsonl` containing `{\"b\":2}\\n`\n- `$T/bad.jsonl` containing `{\"x\":1}\\n{oops}\\n`\n\nB below means `node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\".\nRun `B $T/bad.jsonl $T/a.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl` and also `B $T/bad.jsonl` on its own.\n- PASS: every file named is checked. Each bad row produces one stdout line of the form `<file>:<line>: <error>`, with the file argument as given. The lines appear in the order the files were named. A file named twice is reported twice. The single-file output is in the same format as before (`$T/bad.jsonl:2: <JSON.parse message>`).\n- FAIL: a named file is skipped, the lines are out of argument order, or the format differs from that.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\".\nRun `printf '{oops}\\n' | B $T/a.jsonl -`, then `printf '{\"ok\":1}\\n' | B -`, then `printf '{}\\n' | B - -; echo $?`.\n- PASS: the first prints a line starting `-:1: `. The second prints nothing about a bad row. The third exits with status 2.\n- FAIL: stdin is not read, it is labelled anything other than `-`, or `- -` does not exit 2.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\".\nRun these and record each exit status, stdout and stderr:\n- `B $T/a.jsonl $T/b.jsonl`\n- `B $T/a.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl $T/b.jsonl`\n- `B`\n- `B $T/a.jsonl $T/missing.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl`\nPASS: the statuses are 0, 1, 2 and 2. In the last run, stderr has an error, and stdout still contains the bad-row lines for `$T/bad.jsonl`, which shows the other files were checked and reported. FAIL: any status differs, the last run has no stderr error, or the last run stops before reporting `$T/bad.jsonl`.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\".\nRun `git diff <merge-base with main>..HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js` (find the merge base with `git merge-base origin/main HEAD`).\n- PASS: the lint function's code and behaviour are unchanged (an empty diff is the clear pass).\n- FAIL: lint's signature, body or behaviour changed.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\".\nRun `npm test --workspace packages/jsonl-lint`, then read the test files under packages/jsonl-lint/test/ and packages/jsonl-lint/README.md.\n- PASS: the tests run under node:test and pass, and between them there are tests for all of these cases: two clean files; one bad file among several; stdin via `-` with clean input; stdin via `-` with bad input; a missing file among good ones; two `-` arguments; no arguments. The README documents the multi-file usage, including `-` for stdin.\n- FAIL: any case has no test, the tests fail, or the README does not document the new usage.\n\nCheck 6. Expect line: \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\".\nRun `gh pr view <pr> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test --json body,headRefOid,statusCheckRollup` and `npm test` at the repository root.\n- PASS: there is one PR on that repo with the change. Its body contains `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Root `npm test` passes. The `test` workflow check at the head commit has concluded success.\n- FAIL: the body lacks that line, `npm test` fails, or the test workflow is not successful at the head.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Check that stderr for `- -` and for no arguments contains a usage message.\n- Check that the `-` usage error is raised before any file is read.\n- Check that the order of stdout lines inside one file follows row order.\n- Run the existing single-file tests in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js to confirm nothing regressed."}